s Against a Defendant Can Impact Jurors' Memories for Evidence That Was Never Presented at Trial\n\nDescription:\nThe current experiment investigated how inducing a negative bias against a defendant can influence mock-jurors' verdicts and memory for the evidence presented at trial. Participants (N = 234) watched a simulated trial in which the defendant was either described as a dangerous gang member, or gang affiliation was never mentioned. After deliberating in groups, jurors rendered verdicts and were administered a remember/know memory test. The memory test included evidence items that were and were not presented in the simulated trial. Further, the remember/know questionnaire was used to differentiate between feelings of familiarity (i.e., know) and actual false memories (i.e., remember). Results demonstrated that participants in the gang condition were more likely to falsely recognize evidence related to the defendant's previous criminal conduct and previous convictions. Many of those who falsely recognized this evidence were also likely to report remembering this evidence, indicating that many participants experience false memories. The current findings suggest that gang evidence is just as prejudicial as presenting evidence of a defendant's previous criminal acts, which is often prohibited in court. Based on these findings, it is recommended that the courts bifurcate gang evidence whenever possible. This would improve due-process by not allowing gang evidence to bias jurors.\n\nEvaluate whether this book satisfies the user's search intent.\n\nReturn:\n\n* score (0, 1, or 2)\n* concise reason\n", 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'schema': {'description': 'Structured relevance judgment for a single (query, book) pair.\n\nThe ``reason`` field is mandatory so every judgment can be audited\nor used for fine-tuning the judge model later.', 'properties': {'score': {'description': 'Relevance score: 0 = not relevant, 1 = somewhat relevant, 2 = highly relevant.', 'title': 'Score', 'type': 'integer'}, 'reason': {'description': 'Concise explanation (1-3 sentences) justifying the score. Used for auditing and debugging.', 'title': 'Reason', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['score', 'reason'], 'title': 'RelevanceScore', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False}, 'name': 'RelevanceScore', 'strict': True}}, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
