 Adventures of Rivella: A Woman's Quest\n\nDescription:\nAs a female author of amatory fiction in early eighteenth-century England, Delarivier Manley would not only be denounced in the decades following her life and career for having written licentious stories of sexual conspiracies, but due to the amatory genre's tie to the heroic romance, she would, with the emergence of a prescriptive and domestic realism in mid eighteenth-century, mainly be remembered as a trivial non-novelistic author whose stories carried little value.This essay aims to explore how romance, and Manley as a Tory (royalist) in her political leaning, was mainly negated in mid century due to a cultural shift in England where romance, in the eyes of a rising middle-class and parliamentary government, came to be seen as a backward looking genre in favor of absolute monarchy and aristocratic idealism. This essay aims to tease out how Manley, when remaining a tie to an archaic genre (romance) in her otherwise very realistic biographical work The Adventures of Rivella, does so not because she as a woman was expected to, but because a personal and generic inconsistency allowed her to suggest England's shift from old to new world in a post-revolutionary era as equally non-linear and uncertain as the story she writes about herself. When Manley's tie to romance becomes a stylistic choice rather than novelistic error, we are forced to reconsider the historical and novelistic value of a woman who were thought of as writing little less than trivial stories as scandalous as her own life.\n\nEvaluate whether this book satisfies the user's search intent.\n\nReturn:\n\n* score (0, 1, or 2)\n* concise reason\n", 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'schema': {'description': 'Structured relevance judgment for a single (query, book) pair.\n\nThe ``reason`` field is mandatory so every judgment can be audited\nor used for fine-tuning the judge model later